# Apupiwe: reference
<!-- Generated from spec/reference.toml by tools/spec.py build. Edit the spec, not this file. -->

<span id="r-0-1"></span>This document states what the language is. The reasons for each part are in [rationale.md](rationale.md), its history is in [design-log.md](design-log.md), and the word lists are in the [data folder](https://github.com/LilijoySkyseeker/apupiwe/tree/main/data).

<span id="r-0-2"></span>The language calls itself **apu pi we** "everyone's language", said [ˈapu pi we], roughly AH-poo pee weh, with only the first syllable stressed. Its name in English and other languages is one word, Apupiwe, said the same way. Any language is named with **apu pi** followed by whose language it is.

<span id="r-0-3"></span>Apupiwe is a designed language for people who speak it natively, within the world's existing cultures. Its own grammar is built for native speakers. Where it meets a culture's conventions, such as clocks, calendars, units, names and customs, it carries them. Where conventions conflict and one must be chosen, it follows the international standard.

<span id="r-0-4"></span>What the language is for, and how its criteria are weighed against each other, is set out in [the goal](goal.md).

---

## 1. Principles

1. <span id="r-1-1"></span>The common case costs nothing. Statements, ordinary belief, an unknown source, the topic, and present or unspecified time are unmarked. Frequent meanings have the shortest words.
2. <span id="r-1-2"></span>Heads come first, and position carries role. The order is subject, verb, object. A content word that describes a noun or a verb follows it. Words never move to show their role.
3. <span id="r-1-3"></span>Operators come before what they cover (section 6).
4. <span id="r-1-4"></span>Heavy goes last. A noun's own modifiers come before its **pi** "of" phrase, and long clauses go to the end. Nothing may follow a relative clause in its phrase.
5. <span id="r-1-5"></span>Doubling widens or strengthens any word except a digit in a number: **su su** emphatic "not", **wu wu** extra deference, **yo yo** emergency, **genso genso** "the whole turn is not literal", **nen nen** "each other" (a set phrase), **feko feko** "exactly so". Doubled **su** is always emphasis and never means "not not".
6. <span id="r-1-6"></span>Emphasis, politeness, respect and stance have words of their own: **ka** and **ten** for emphasis, **wu** for politeness, **lanno** for respect, and the stance words of section 7.
7. <span id="r-1-7"></span>A precise way always exists, but it need not always be used. Anything the listener can supply may be left out (section 4.5), and any ambiguity can be removed by saying more.

## 2. Sounds

- <span id="r-2-1"></span>The vowels are a e i o u. In the reference pronunciation, every syllable keeps its full vowel.
- <span id="r-2-2"></span>There are 13 consonants: p b t d k g · m n · f s · l w y. They are made at the lips (p b m w, and f with lip and teeth), with the tongue tip (t d n s l), with the tongue body (y) and with the back of the tongue (k g). In the reference pronunciation p t k are aspirated at the start of a word, with a puff of air as in English *pin*, and b d g are voiced, so the pairs differ both ways: speakers of languages that tell them apart by voicing and those that tell them apart by aspiration both hear the difference. Unaspirated p t k are a harmless variant.
- <span id="r-2-3"></span>A syllable has the shape (C)V(n). A syllable with no consonant before its vowel (as in **apu**, **ewo**) occurs only at the start of a word. The only consonant that can end a syllable is n.
- <span id="r-2-4"></span>Words fall into three types by sound:
  - grammar words have one unstressed syllable (table in section 13), except the two-syllable operators listed after that table, which are stressed on the first like content words;
  - the digits 0–5 have one stressed syllable;
  - every other word has two or more syllables and is stressed on the first.
- <span id="r-2-5"></span>Words never change form. There are no endings, no agreement, no case and no gender.
- <span id="r-2-6"></span>Stress is a higher pitch and a little more force (loudness) on the stressed syllable, stepping down after it. The force keeps stress audible where pitch is lost, as in a whisper or a song. In the reference pronunciation stress does not lengthen the syllable, and the rhythm is syllable-timed.
- <span id="r-2-7"></span>Across a phrase, each stressed syllable is a little lower than the one before (declination), and pitch resets after a pause.
- <span id="r-2-8"></span>There are no tones. Pitch carries only intonation and emotion. A question needs no rising tone, because its words make it a question, but rising anyway is fine.
- <span id="r-2-9"></span>These accent variants are harmless: they never turn one word into another. p t k with or without the puff of air; b d g softened between vowels in an unstressed syllable, as in Spanish *cada*; k and g sounding like "ch" and "j" before i and e; l dark, or as a tap as in Spanish *pero*; f made with both lips; w made with the lip and teeth, as in Dutch *wat*; s as "sh"; a vowel plus n as a nasal vowel; and a catch in the throat (a glottal stop) where a word ending in a vowel meets one starting with a vowel. The reference pronunciation uses the plain sounds, keeps every vowel full, and doesn't lengthen stressed syllables, but none of that is a rule.
- <span id="r-2-10"></span>**mm** "heard you", **huh?** "say again", and any other interjection or hesitation sound speakers use are outside the sound system and carry no grammar.
- <span id="r-2-11"></span>Native content words never begin with an open syllable identical to a digit (**mu ni sa de wo ma**). Loans are exempt.
- <span id="r-2-12"></span>No two content words, and no two short words (grammar words and digits) that can stand in the same place, differ only by sounds a harmless variant (2.9) brings together: b and w, d and l, and g and w before o and u, where softening applies (between vowels, in an unstressed syllable: a one-syllable grammar word, or a later syllable of a longer word); g and y, or k and s, before i and e, anywhere. So a speaker with any of these accents is understood, and no contrast hangs on the sound changes these variants begin. A new grammar word also never differs only by its vowel from a grammar word that can stand in its place, since unstressed vowels weaken.

## 3. Writing

- <span id="r-3-1"></span>The letters are a b d e f g i k l m n o p s t u w y, one per sound, with no accents. Digits never appear inside a word. Spelling follows the standard pronunciation, and neither the harmless variants nor stress are written.
- <span id="r-3-2"></span>Lowercase is the standard. Capitals carry no meaning and are harmless.
- <span id="r-3-3"></span>These marks carry grammar:

  | Mark | Use |
  |---|---|
  | `,` | a pause (section 4.6) |
  | `.` | end of a sentence; between digits, the decimal point |
  | `" "` | around quoted speech |

  `?` and `!` are allowed. In the reference variety the words already show what they would mark, so they carry no grammar of their own. `-` and `:` are not used in the language's own words.
- <span id="r-3-4"></span>Some things pass through as written: chat abbreviations and interjections (ok, lol), digits, unit symbols (`5 km`, `20 °C`, `30%`), the local clock notation (`21:30`, `9:30 pm`), ISO dates (`2026-10-10`), letters used as labels, links, email addresses, @handles, #tags, emoji, code, file names, and personal and brand names (section 11). Unit symbols are read aloud by their borrowed names (9.3) and clock notation in the speaker's clock style (8.3). Other notation is read as the speaker's culture reads it.
- <span id="r-3-5"></span>Ordinary writing uses digits for numbers. This reference spells numbers out only to show how they are said. Digits are grouped in threes from the decimal point with a space (`1 250 000`), and `.` is the decimal point.
- <span id="r-3-6"></span>Words are written apart. A phrase said fast keeps its spaces until a fused form actually changes sound and becomes a new word.

## 4. The sentence

### 4.1 Template

<span id="r-4-1-1"></span>A sentence fills these slots in this order. Only the verb, or a fragment (4.5), is required.

<span id="r-4-1-2"></span>

| # | Slot | Contents | Section |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Calling someone | a person word (a name, a title, or a single role or kin word), then a pause | 10.1 |
| 2 | Opener | a linker to the previous sentence (**bo yin** "so"), then a relation phrase that sets the scene (time, place) or a topic **pi X**, then an **if**/**when** clause, each followed by a pause | 4.3 |
| 3 | Speech act | one particle; **genso** or **ka** may come before it | 7.1 |
| 4 | Politeness | **wu**, after the particle or in its place | 10.2 |
| 5 | Clause operators | confidence **pe** + number, source **wanme**/**inwun**/**wemen** | 6.1 |
| 6 | Subject | a noun phrase (section 5) | |
| 7 | Phrase operators, habit, aspect | e.g. **su**, **pon**, **manni**, **ben** | 6.2, 8.1 |
| 8 | Verb | one verb, or verb + verb; with no object, a bare word right after the verb says how | 4.2 |
| 9 | Objects | recipient, then thing | 4.2 |
| 10 | Relation phrases | **no**, **len**, **don**, **nan**, **fan**, **fun** … + noun phrase or clause; **fun** + a word says how | 4.4, 4.2 |
| 11 | Trailing clause | **me**, **bo**, **sin**, **pu** … clause | 4.4 |
| 12 | Stance | a pause, then **bi**, **yan**, **buni**, **donon** … | 7.2 |
| 13 | Calling someone | a pause, then a person word | 10.1 |

### 4.2 The verb and its objects

- <span id="r-4-2-1"></span>For any content word, what you mean decides the clear form:
  - for a state ("is big", "is closed"), use **gun** + word: **guyi gun tugu** "the house is big". A feeling named by an action word is said with the plain verb: **wan nuta** "I'm afraid" (I fear);
  - for an event ("walks", "is walking", "walked"), use the word as the verb, with an aspect word when precision matters: **ewo li tedo** "the person is walking", **ewo manni tedo** "the person (usually) walks", **ewo ben tedo** "the person walked";
  - for a description of a noun ("the person who walks", "the house that is big"), use **gi**: **ewo gi tedo**, **guyi gi gun tugu**.
- <span id="r-4-2-2"></span>A bare noun followed by a word (**guyi tugu**, **ewo tedo**) may be a description or a statement. Context decides, and the clear forms above remove any doubt.
- <span id="r-4-2-3"></span>Any content word can take any role by its position. As an object, or with a derivation marker, it is a noun (**mosu** "apologise / an apology").
- <span id="r-4-2-4"></span>How something is done is said with **fun** "like", an ordinary relation phrase after the verb and its objects: **nawi pasu fun yobe** "eat food quickly". A bare word right after an object describes the object: **nawi pasu yobe** "eat fast food". With no object, a bare word right after the verb describes the action: **oyi tesin** "say calmly".
- <span id="r-4-2-5"></span>With two objects, the recipient comes first and then the thing: **wiwa ba nuta** "teach you fear".
- <span id="r-4-2-6"></span>When one verb follows another and the first takes an object (want, try, need, must, can, start, stop, teach, see, like, help…), the second verb is that object: **walo alun** "try to break". Otherwise the second verb describes the first: **doyu pume** "sit thinking", **wan isi yinmi tanke** "I go through the forest" (go passing the forest; there is no word for "through"). Phrases and clauses after a second verb that is an object belong to it, the nearest verb: **momu isi len guyi** "want to go home".
- <span id="r-4-2-7"></span>**tula** "do; make" followed by a result and then an object means "make (it) so": **tula upe yin** "fix this". Followed by an object and then a **fun** phrase saying how, it means "do (it) so": **tula yin fun upe** "do this well".
- <span id="r-4-2-8"></span>A command uses **ti**, or **lo** for a request. In casual speech a bare verb with no subject is also a command (**obu!** "come!"); **ti** is the precise form, and "don't" always needs it: **ti su kusa** "don't leave". Otherwise a sentence without a particle is a statement.

### 4.3 Openers

- <span id="r-4-3-6"></span>A linker is a word or phrase at the start that ties the sentence to the one before it, such as **bo yin** "so", **fen** "but" or **nun** "and also".
- <span id="r-4-3-1"></span>Any relation phrase may open the sentence, followed by a pause. A time or place phrase there sets the scene: **no minfi, …** "at noon, …". An opening phrase changes no roles.

 succeeded in 0ms:
- <span id="r-4-3-2"></span>A topic is **pi** + a noun phrase at the start, meaning "as for X": **pi tale la, win kuwi guyi, yidu bun la.** "As for the cars, the caretaker takes them." At the start, **pi** has no other reading, and the topic runs to its pause, or to **kiyu** "end of phrase", so it can be a list: **pi upe nun yomo,** "as for good and bad,".
- <span id="r-4-3-3"></span>An if/when clause is **sin** "if", **pu** "while/when", **kon** "after", **ya** "before" or **bo** "because", followed by a clause.
- <span id="r-4-3-4"></span>When several of these open a sentence, they come in this order: linker, scene or topic, if/when clause. The speech-act particle follows them.
- <span id="r-4-3-5"></span>Only a person word (4.1) calls someone. A longer noun phrase before a pause is the subject (4.6), and a fronted relation phrase or **pi** phrase is a scene or topic phrase.

### 4.4 Clauses and relation phrases

- <span id="r-4-4-1"></span>A relation phrase (**no**, **len**, **don**, **nan**, **fan**, **ya**, **kon**, **fun**, **pu**, **bo** "because of", and the two-syllable **anko** "until" and **nuwen** "without") at clause level attaches to the clause's verb. A relation phrase right after a noun may instead describe that noun: **osa nan tanun tufun** "tea with lemon" (not after a pronoun, and not **fun**, which after an object says how). To be precise, use a relative clause for the noun (**osa gi ibe tanun tufun**, **sola gi gun no fide pi yolan** "the road that is above the river") or put the phrase first for the verb (**nan tanun tufun, wan koye osa**). In comparisons and quantities (9.3), **don**, **len** and **fun** belong to their number or adjective. After a time, **kon** and **ya** belong to that time (8.2): **wan isi no wuma kon dili ni** "I go the day after tomorrow". **bo** with a noun phrase gives **bo yu** "why" and **bo yin** "so" (because of this).
- <span id="r-4-4-2"></span>A relation word may drop its object when context supplies it, as in **kon** "after that" and **ya** "beforehand". It then ends its clause or comes before a pause.
- <span id="r-4-4-3"></span>A relation word can take a clause (**kon mon la yidu ewo kin** "after they took someone") or an action noun (**don kusa** "from leaving").
- <span id="r-4-4-4"></span>A relative clause attaches to the nearest noun before it. A time, reason or condition clause at the *start* of a sentence belongs to the main clause. At the *end*, it belongs to the innermost clause before it, unless a pause closes that clause first (**kiyu** closes relative clauses but not a trailing clause like this one, 5.3): **pu leso, wan ben miya ewo gi sigo** "in the morning, I watched the person who came back"; **wan ben miya ewo gi sigo pu leso** "I watched the person who came back in the morning". In the same way, a phrase or clause after verbs joined by **nun**, **lu** or **fen** belongs to the last of them, and at the start it covers them all: **mon doku nun nawi no guyi** "he works, and eats at home"; **no guyi, mon doku nun nawi** "at home, he works and eats".
- <span id="r-4-4-5"></span>**nun** "and", **lu** "or" and **fen** "but" join nouns, verbs, modifiers or clauses. Nouns in a list are joined with **nun**: **mole nun sine** "mother and father". **lu X lu Y** means "either X or Y, not both".
- <span id="r-4-4-7"></span>Verbs joined by **nun**, **lu** or **fen** are separate predicates. Operators and aspect words before one of them cover only that verb, and an object after the last verb belongs to it: **mon su nawi nun fimo** "she doesn't eat, and she sleeps"; **wan nagu nun yidu bun** "I see (it) and take it".
- <span id="r-4-4-6"></span>A quote is framed by a say-verb, and in writing it goes in quotation marks. It is its own sentence with its own speech-act particle. Broken-off speech just stops.

### 4.5 Leaving things out

- <span id="r-4-5-1"></span>Anything the listener can supply from context may be left out: the subject, the object, the object of a relation word or the verb. An answer or follow-up can be just the new part: **nufi yu? osin?** "what time? again?"; **nagu ba la no dili mena ufo.** "see you all Saturday".
- <span id="r-4-5-2"></span>**gun** with no subject is "(it) is", like any verb with its subject left out: **gun nasi** "(it's) money". With **gun** left out too, a bare state is fine: **ten upe.** "very good." "There is" is **ibe** "have" with no subject (4.7).
- <span id="r-4-5-3"></span>When leaving something out could be unclear, say it.
- <span id="r-4-5-4"></span>An action word with no object is read by its subject. A subject that can't act undergoes the action: **beya ben tapi** "the door closed", **fileni ben alun** "the cup broke". A subject that can act does it to something the context supplies: **fo mara ben ibe fileni. mon ben alun.** "Mara had a cup. She broke it." To be precise, say the object, or use **nen** "self".

### 4.6 Pauses

<span id="r-4-6-1"></span>A pause, written as a comma, goes wherever a phrase could otherwise run on. It belongs in these places:
- <span id="r-4-6-2"></span>after someone called at the start, and before someone called at the end;
- <span id="r-4-6-3"></span>after an opener (linker, scene, topic, if/when clause);
- <span id="r-4-6-4"></span>after a subject that contains a clause or a phrasal derived word, before its verb (**win sanwa gi … ben pofi, kifa unfu …**);
- <span id="r-4-6-5"></span>between a recipient and the thing, where it helps: it is optional, and recommended when the thing is several words long or has a relative clause;
- <span id="r-4-6-6"></span>between clauses joined by **nun**, **lu** or **fen** when the first ends in a noun, and the same between joined verbs (4.4.7): **wan nawi pasu, nun fimo** "I eat food, and sleep";
- <span id="r-4-6-7"></span>before a stance word, and before the choice tag **yu** (4.8);
- <span id="r-4-6-8"></span>between a larger and a smaller calendar unit (**dunan mena yawu, dili mena tuli**);
- <span id="r-4-6-9"></span>after a relative clause, to close it before more material (or **kiyu**, 5.3).

<span id="r-4-6-10"></span>In casual writing, pauses that context makes obvious may be dropped.

### 4.7 "Is": gun

- <span id="r-4-7-1"></span>**gun** goes before a state: **guyi gun tugu** "the house is big". Without it, **guyi tugu** is normally read as "the big house".
- <span id="r-4-7-2"></span>**gun** goes before a noun: **mon gun tiwu** "she is a child".
- <span id="r-4-7-3"></span>**gun** goes before a place phrase: **mon gun no guyi** "he is at home".
- <span id="r-4-7-4"></span>**gun** before an action word gives the state of whoever undergoes the action: **gun tapi** "is closed", **mon gun kuwi** "she is protected", **wan gun nuta** "I am feared". This holds for every action word, feelings included; the one who feels takes the plain verb (**wan nuta** "I'm afraid").
- <span id="r-4-7-5"></span>**gun** before a **me** clause means "is that …".
- <span id="r-4-7-6"></span>**gun** with no subject is always "is" or "to be", with the subject understood: **momu gun tiwu** "want to be a child", **dan ibe sogan fan gun kuwi** "everyone has the right to be protected", **mon obu nun gun simi** "she came and is sick".
- <span id="r-4-7-7"></span>"There is" is **ibe** "have" with no subject, and **ibe** with no subject always means "there is": **ibe pasu** "there's food", **su ibe pasu** "there's no food", **fen, ibe yefi fefa** "but there are many things", **sin ibe pasu** "if there is food". To say someone has it, name them: **wan ibe pasu** "I have food".

### 4.8 Questions and answers

- <span id="r-4-8-1"></span>A yes/no question starts with **bi**. A **bi** at the end of a clause is a tag, "…, right?".
- <span id="r-4-8-2"></span>Question words stay where the answer goes and need no **bi**: **ba momu me wan oyi yu.** "you want me to say what?"
- <span id="r-4-8-3"></span>Question words are built from **yu**: **yu** what; **ewo yu** who; **X yu** which X; **no fela yu** where; **no nufi yu** when; **bo yu** why; **fun yu** how; **wifu yu** how many/much; **pi ewo yu** whose. **wifu yu** follows its noun the way a number does: **ewo wifu yu** "how many people".
- <span id="r-4-8-4"></span>To offer a choice, ask **bi … lu …** "either?", or ask **… lu …, yu** "which?" with a pause before **yu** so it isn't read as "which X". That second kind is answered by echoing the choice.
- <span id="r-4-8-5"></span>An indirect question starts its **me** clause with **bi**, with or without a question word: **wan su loya me bi mon obu** "I don't know whether she's coming", **wan su loya me bi ewo yu obu** "I don't know who is coming". Without **bi**, a question word anywhere in the sentence makes the whole sentence a question: **ba momu me wan oyi yu** "you want me to say what?"
- <span id="r-4-8-6"></span>Answers echo the verb, since there are no words for yes and no: "Aren't you coming?" → **obu** / **su obu**. A question with **gun** is answered by echoing what follows **gun**: "Is the house big?" → **tugu** / **su tugu**. The short answer is **feko** "true" or **su feko** "not true": it says whether the question's statement, exactly as asked, is true, so after a negative question **feko** agrees with the negative: **bi ba su obu** "aren't you coming?" → **feko** "right, I'm not". The echo is the precise answer. To acknowledge, **mm** says you heard, an echo says you understood, and **du** + verb says "will do". **ok** agrees to a proposal or instruction ("let's go" → **ok**) and doesn't answer a yes/no question.
- <span id="r-4-8-7"></span>A lost short word usually leaves a normal sentence, so nobody notices. When it matters, say it twice: **su su** for a "not" that must get through, a yes/no question ended with the tag **bi** as well (**bi ba obu, bi** "are you coming, are you?"), a warning repeated whole, and the answer echoed back in full (**wan obu** "I am coming"). In noise or across a distance, speak this way by default.

## 5. The noun phrase

### 5.1 Template

<span id="r-5-1-1"></span>A noun phrase runs in this order: noun, **wi** (5.2.12), content modifiers, number, **la**, determiner, **pi** phrase, relative clause, as in **guyi tugu sa la yin pi fo mara gi …**. Titles and **lanno** come before the noun (10.1), and phrase operators come before the whole phrase (6.2).

### 5.2 Parts

- <span id="r-5-2-1"></span>Describing words follow the noun: **guyi tugu** "big house". For a clear description, use **gi** (5.3).
- <span id="r-5-2-2"></span>Gender is a modifier, stated once when it matters: **tiwu futa** "daughter", **win wungu futa** "the leader, a woman".
- <span id="r-5-2-11"></span>Describing words after a noun each describe the noun: **wan ibe lipo wiwi buda** "I have a red, dark cloth". **tan** between two describing words makes the second describe the first: **wan ibe lipo wiwi tan buda** "I have a dark-red cloth". It joins only the word before it and the word after it, and works after **gun** too: **lipo gun wiwi tan buda** "the cloth is dark red".
- <span id="r-5-2-12"></span>**wi** right after a noun or a name adds affection, "dear little": **poka wi** "doggy", **fo mara wi** "dear Mara". It comes before the noun's describing words: **poka wi tugu** "the dear big dog". As a grammar word it also ends a name, so a content word can follow it with no pause: **fo mara wi obu** "dear Mara is coming".
- <span id="r-5-2-3"></span>Noun + noun is used only for gender and for the compounds in `compounds.tsv`. Other relations use **pi** or a relative clause.
- <span id="r-5-2-4"></span>Numbers and times can stand alone as noun phrases: **yawu gun upe** "ten is fine", **wuma gun upe** "tomorrow is fine".
- <span id="r-5-2-5"></span>A noun followed by a number is a count (**dili oma** "seven days"). A noun + **mena** + number is a label (**pata mena sa** "document 2"), and a label also gives a place in order: **bifo mena de** "the third time", **ewo mena ni** "the first person". "Last" is **pi fugi**, "of the end": **bifo pi fugi** "the last time". With a number before **pi** "of" and a noun after it, the phrase is a part of a set: **sa pi ewo de** "two of the three people". Two numbers joined by **pi** are a fraction (9.2.7).
- <span id="r-5-2-6"></span>The plural word **la** is optional. A count or context can show plurality.
- <span id="r-5-2-7"></span>There are no articles. Whether something is known or new is left to context, or shown with **yin**, **fin**, **kin** or a number.
- <span id="r-5-2-8"></span>Determiners (table in section 13) and **mesun** "each" follow the noun: **guyi yin** "this house", **ewo kin** "someone", **yefi kin** "something", **sen nume mesun** "each home". A determiner right after a noun or pronoun belongs to it. So when the thing given is a pronoun or a determiner standing alone, mark the recipient with **len**, the normal form there: **wan iko fin len mon** "I told that to him" (**wan iko mon fin** is "I told that-him"). A determiner may stand alone as a noun phrase: **we** "all", **fe** "some", **din** "none". A part of a whole is a determiner or number + **pi** + noun: **ye pi silu la** "some of the photos".
- <span id="r-5-2-9"></span>**pi** "of" links a noun to a noun: **guyi pi fo mara** "Mara's house". Each **pi** attaches to the nearest noun before it, and its phrase is the nearest single noun after it, so a list joined by **nun** or **lu** is outside it: **enpa pi pume nun lite pi nubige** is "freedom of thought, and religion". To cover every item, repeat the head: **enpa pi pume nun enpa pi lite pi nubige** "freedom of thought and freedom of religion".
- <span id="r-5-2-10"></span>"One's own" is **pi nen**: **wan ibe guyi pi nen** "I have my own house". Since a **pi** phrase holds a single noun, two owners of one thing take a relative clause: **guyi gi fo mara nun wan ibe** "Mara's and my house".

### 5.3 Relative clauses

<span id="r-5-3-1"></span>A relative clause is **gi** + clause, placed after the noun. The noun fills the role missing from the clause:
- <span id="r-5-3-2"></span>subject: **ewo gi tedo** "the person who walks";
- <span id="r-5-3-3"></span>object: **natun gi ba la yaga** "the papers you put";
- <span id="r-5-3-4"></span>place or time, with the relation word at the end of the clause: **fela gi wan nume no** "the place where I live". The precise form keeps a pronoun after the relation word: **fela gi wan nume no bun** "the place I live in (it)";
- <span id="r-5-3-6"></span>any other relation, the same way: **wonnun gi wan fonsu nan** "the tool I cut with";
- <span id="r-5-3-7"></span>"whose", with **pi** + the pronoun that fits the noun (**pi mon**, **pi bun**, **pi mon la**) inside the clause pointing back to it: **ewo gi guyi pi mon gun tugu** "the person whose house is big", **guyi gi beya pi bun gun tapi** "the house whose door is closed".

<span id="r-5-3-8"></span>If both the subject and the object are missing, the noun is the subject: **ewo gi nagu** "the person who sees". For "the person I see", keep the subject: **ewo gi wan nagu**.

<span id="r-5-3-5"></span>A relative clause attaches to the nearest noun or pronoun before it, so after a **pi** phrase it describes the **pi** noun. To describe the head instead, put a quality before the **pi** phrase or use a new sentence. A pause, or the word **kiyu** "end of phrase", closes it, along with every relative clause still open: **wan ben miya ewo gi sigo kiyu pu leso** "I watched the person who came back, in the morning". **kiyu** is optional; use it where a missed pause could mislead. It closes only the phrases that run on: relative clauses, phrasal derived words (5.4) and topics (4.3). A trailing clause (4.4.4) is closed by a pause. Because one **kiyu** closes every open relative clause, a phrase can't be attached to an outer relative clause while an inner one is open; the precise way is to restructure, for example with a second sentence.

### 5.4 Derived words

<span id="r-5-4-1"></span>Four markers before a verb or verb phrase name a role in the action:

<span id="r-5-4-2"></span>

| Marker | Role | Example |
|---|---|---|
| **win** | the one who does it | **win febo** "one who answers" |
| **gen** | the thing done or made | **gen iko** "what is told" = news |

 succeeded in 0ms:
| 10 | 100 | 1000 | million | billion | point | minus | × 10 to the |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **yawu** | **loki** | **munno** | **tiya** | **siwin** | **mimi** | **guti** | **unun** |

### 9.2 Reading numbers

- <span id="r-9-2-1"></span>The place words are **yawu** 10, **loki** 100, **munno** 1000, **tiya** million and **siwin** billion. The last three are also group words. In the quantity reading, place words come in descending order, each preceded by its digit. A 1 before a place word is dropped, and zero places are skipped: 15 **yawu ma**, 50 **ma yawu**, 42 **wo yawu sa**, 105 **loki ma**. A group word takes a whole number before it: 250 000 = **sa loki ma yawu munno**.
- <span id="r-9-2-2"></span>The digit reading says one digit at a time. It is used whenever the exact digits matter more than the size: for identifiers, phone numbers and codes (in groups of three from the right), and for years and 24-hour clock times (as one four-digit group).
- <span id="r-9-2-3"></span>Labels (noun + **mena** + number) under 100 use the quantity reading (**dili mena yawu wo** "day 14"). Larger labels use the digit reading.
- <span id="r-9-2-4"></span>Decimals use **mimi** "point": 1.5 = **ni mimi ma**. **guti** means minus. With **unun** "× 10 to the", **de unun guti ufo** = 3 × 10⁻⁶.
- <span id="r-9-2-5"></span>Arithmetic aloud is a reading of notation, like spelling aloud, not an ordinary sentence: read the signs in the order written, + **ginni**, − **guti**, × **bifo**, ÷ **gonwu**, = **gun**: **sa ginni de gun ma** "2 + 3 = 5", **de bifo wo gun yawu sa** "3 × 4 = 12", **ufo gonwu sa gun de** "6 ÷ 2 = 3". **guti** before a number makes it negative and between two numbers means minus; a list keeps its pauses (**ma, guti de** "5, −3").
- <span id="r-9-2-6"></span>A rate is said with **mesun** "each" after the unit, joined by the relation word that fits: **wan nawi bifo sa no dili mesun** "I eat twice a day" (on each day), **wan neke de yefi len ewo mesun** "I give three to each person". Unit symbols such as `60 km/h` pass through as written and are read with their borrowed names (9.3).
- <span id="r-9-2-7"></span>A fraction is a number, **pi**, and the number of equal parts: **ni pi de** ⅓, **de pi wo** ¾, **sa pi wo** or **timon** ½. Two numbers joined by **pi** are always a fraction. A fraction of something takes **pi** again: **sa pi de pi ewo la** "two thirds of the people", **wan nawi de pi wo pi pasu** "I eat three quarters of the food". A part of a counted set puts the noun after **pi** instead: **sa pi ewo de** "two of the three people". Exact or long fractions can use decimals (9.2.4) or percent, **lonwon**.

### 9.3 Amounts and comparison

- <span id="r-9-3-1"></span>For "about", put **fun** before the number: **ewo fun de yawu** "about 30 people".
- <span id="r-9-3-2"></span>A range is **don** X **len** Y. "At least" is **konsin** X, and "at most" is **su ke don** X.
- <span id="r-9-3-3"></span>"Enough" is **tonya**.
- <span id="r-9-3-4"></span>A comparison is **ke** + quality + **don** + standard: **ke tugu don guyi fin** "bigger than that house". For "most", compare with everything: **ke tugu don we** "biggest" (bigger than all). For "less", use **ke** with the opposite (**ke meli** "smaller"). For "as … as", use **fun** (**tugu fun bun** "as big as it").
- <span id="r-9-3-5"></span>Measurements use the metric system's unit names, borrowed as one set and built the way the system builds them: a prefix (**kilo**, **senti**, **mili**) run together with a unit (**metulu** metre, **gulamu** gram, **litulu** litre): **kilometulu** kilometre, **kilogulamu** kilogram, **sentimetulu** centimetre. In everyday speech kilogram is **kilo**, the short form most of the world uses. The unit comes first, like any counted noun: **kilometulu ma** "5 km".

## 10. People

### 10.1 Calling someone, titles, respect

- <span id="r-10-1-1"></span>You can call someone with a person word (a name, a title, or a single role or kin word) at the start (slot 1, for attention) or at the end (slot 13, to soften): **fo mara, ti obu.** / **ti obu, fo mara.**
- <span id="r-10-1-2"></span>Titles go before the name with no comma, and work both in address and in reference: **win wiwa fo mara** "Teacher Mara"; **win wiwa fo mara ben oyi** "Teacher Mara said".
- <span id="r-10-1-3"></span>**lanno** is the title of respect, and has no gender. It goes before a name, role or kin word (**lanno fo olusegun** "Mr/Ms Olusegun", **lanno faba pi sine** "Uncle / Aunt (father's sibling)"). It can stand alone in address (**lanno, lo wu …** "Sir/Madam, please …"), and alone in reference when context shows who (**lanno ben oyi** "the honoured one said"). It marks respect for the person named. In the reference variety it is a title, not a word for "you".
- <span id="r-10-1-4"></span>There is no apposition. To describe someone, use a title before the name, a relative clause or a new sentence.

### 10.2 Politeness and routines

- <span id="r-10-2-1"></span>**wu** adds politeness or deference and nothing else. It comes after any opener, right after the speech-act particle (**lo wu …** "please …"), or where the particle would be in a statement. **wu wu** adds more.
- <span id="r-10-2-2"></span>There are no polite pronouns and no speech levels. **ba** is "you" to anyone. Respect is carried by **lanno**, titles and **wu**.
- <span id="r-10-2-3"></span>Politeness never changes the act. A polite instruction is **ti wu**, and a polite warning is **yo wu**: **yo wu osa li obu. fon wu kan wupi isi.** "(Warning, politely) the water is coming. (Advice, politely) we need to go."
- <span id="r-10-2-4"></span>In an emergency (**yo yo**), no **wu** is expected.
- <span id="r-10-2-5"></span>Routines are fixed formulas. The greetings are **upe** and **upe leso** "good morning", the farewell is **upe isi** "go well", thanks is **lota** and sorry is **mosu**. With politeness, **wu** comes first: **wu upe leso**.

### 10.3 Reference

- <span id="r-10-3-1"></span>**mon** "he/she" is the person or animal being talked about. **tun** is another one who isn't the topic: **mon nagu tun** "she saw him". A newcomer is **tun**, or is named, until the speaker makes them the topic.
- <span id="r-10-3-2"></span>**bun** "it" is the thing being talked about. **fin** alone is a thing that isn't the topic: **ba momu bun lu fin, yu.** "this one or that one?"
- <span id="r-10-3-3"></span>**yin** or **fin** alone stands for an event, statement or situation. **yin** is nearer (what I'm saying, here and now), and **fin** further (what you said, an earlier event): **fin gun upe** "that's good"; **bo yin** "so". For a recipient, see 5.2.
- <span id="r-10-3-4"></span>**son**, in reported speech or thought, is the person whose words are reported.
- <span id="r-10-3-5"></span>**dan** is anyone, people in general. In the reference variety, **ba** is the listener only; for people in general, use **dan**. An unknown doer is **ewo kin**.
- <span id="r-10-3-6"></span>**nen** is self. As an object it is reflexive (**wan kuwi nen** "I keep myself"). After a noun or pronoun it means "in person" (**mon nen obu** "he came himself").
- <span id="r-10-3-7"></span>**kan** is we including you, and **ta** is we not including you. **mon la** is "they" and **ba la** "you all".
- <span id="r-10-3-8"></span>A pronoun never means the subject of its own clause; for that, use **nen**. The listener finds referents from context. If one could be unclear, name it.

## 11. Names, loans, new words

- <span id="r-11-1"></span>Names take **fo**: **fo mara**. That is the precise form; casual speech often leaves **fo** out when a name can't be taken for a word (**mara ben obu** "Mara came"). A personal or brand name keeps its owner's spelling in lowercase, even with letters the language lacks: **fo denise**, **fo xi jinping**, **fo nguyen**, **fo wi-fi**. Say it as close to its owner's pronunciation as you can. Place names are names too: **fo london**, though an established adapted form may be used instead: **fo landan**. Names aren't used as verbs. A name runs until the next grammar word or pause, so a name of several words is written with no grammar words inside it, and a content word right after a name needs a grammar word or a pause before it: **fo xi jinping ben oyi** "Xi Jinping said". A name that contains a grammar word is closed with **kiyu** instead, and then runs until it: **fo cuerpo de paz kiyu ben obu** "the Peace Corps came". **kiyu** closes an open relative clause, built word or topic first, and a name only when none of those is open, so in **guyi gi fo mara ben miya kiyu gun tugu** "the house that Mara saw is big" it closes the clause and the name is **mara**. A name that needs **kiyu** therefore can't stand inside a relative clause; say it first, as a topic, or in its own sentence.
- <span id="r-11-2"></span>The adapted form (`tools/names.py`) is the spoken fallback for a name and the form of every loan. It is made like this:
  - each foreign sound becomes the nearest native sound: r → l, v → b, ʋ → w, z/sh/zh → s, th → f, dh → d, ch → t and j → d (but k and g before i or e, whose harmless variant is the original sound: *gene* **geni**), kh → k; h and glottal sounds disappear; ng → n; length, aspiration and tone are dropped; ə, ʌ, æ → a;
  - two vowels in a row get a glide between them, y after i or e and w otherwise;
  - a consonant that can't stand where it is gets an added **u**;
  - the result has at least two syllables, counting a glide (**tuwu**).
- <span id="r-11-3"></span>The language's own vocabulary borrows only where the world shares a form, taken in that international form: **dolalu** dollar, **kilometulu**, **intelunetu**, **imeyilu**, **kamela**. A loan enters the vocabulary once it is common, and until then near-collisions are tolerated. A loan is identified by its consonants: the vowels added to fit the syllables (the final **u** of **kodu** "code") and the **l** that stands for an r may be said as in the speaker's own form of the loan, and no other word sits where such a variant would land. Speakers also borrow freely from their own languages, as speakers of every language do. A borrowed word takes no **fo** and is used like any word. Say it in its own sounds or the language's, and write it by its sound in the language's letters, as in the adapted form (11.2): **wan nawi susi sa** "I ate two sushi". Casual writing often keeps the source spelling at first (*sushi*); borrowings settle into the language's letters as they're used. A borrowed word can do anything a word can, including be a verb (**wan ben gugalu bun** "I googled it"), while a name with **fo** is never a verb (**wan ben ditun bun no fo google** "I searched for it on Google"). A borrowed word that would sound like a word of the language is reshaped: adapted another way, or replaced by a native word or phrase (**fo** marks only names, which are never verbs). Borrowings that catch on drift toward the language's sounds; `tools/names.py` gives the written form. Native words, built words and phrases can always say the same thing.
- <span id="r-11-4"></span>Computers and interfaces use everyday words: search **ditun**, delete **nuton**, share **peton**, copy **gayen**, add **ginni**, download **dunu**, install **yaga**, turn on/off **selon**/**tapi**, settings **gen finsa la** (`compounds.tsv`); computer is the loan **konpuyutalu**. **doku** "work" covers a machine working.
- <span id="r-11-5"></span>Letters used as labels (apartment 3B) pass through and are read as the speaker's culture reads them.
- To spell aloud for someone of another culture (a name, an email address, a code), say each letter by its word from the international spelling alphabet (ICAO), adapted to this language's sounds: a **alufa**, b **bulabo**, c **saluli**, d **deluta**, e **eko**, f **fokusutulotu**, g **golufu**, h **otelu**, i **indiya**, j **duliyetu**, k **kilo**, l **lima**, m **mayiku**, n **nobenbalu**, o **osuka**, p **papa**, q **kebeku**, r **lomeyo**, s **siyela**, t **tango**, u **yunifolumu**, v **bikuta**, w **wisuki**, x **ekusuleyi**, y **yanki**, z **sulu**. Within one culture its own letter names are fine. **kilo** is also "kilogram" (9.3.5); in spelling it is always a letter, as in the original alphabet.
- Read aloud, an address uses **no** for @, **mimi** for the dot and **komu** for "com": *mara@gmail.com* is **mara no gmail mimi komu**. A hashtag is **asutagu**. Other signs are said with ordinary words: * **segin** "star", & **nun** "and", / **tenen** "line", \ **tenen panwe** "back line", _ **tenen fuso** "line below", - **tenen eten** "short line" (as minus, **guti**).
- <span id="r-11-6"></span>New words are made by the method in [vocabulary-method.md](vocabulary-method.md).

## 12. Conditions and modality

- <span id="r-12-1"></span>A condition is **sin** "if" + clause. **pe** + number after **sin** says how likely the condition is: **sin pe ni** "if (unlikely)". **sin pe mu** "if (and it isn't so)" is counterfactual: **sin pe mu wan ben isi, wan ben nagu mon** "if I had gone, I would have seen her". The main clause is the consequence, and there is no "would".
- <span id="r-12-2"></span>There are three kinds of modality: ability, with **nebi** "can"; obligation, with **kifa** "must" and **wupi** "need to" ("should" is advice, the act **fon**, 7.1); and permission, with **efon** "allow" (**ba gun efon nawi** "you may eat"). There, **gun** + an action word takes a verb as its object, as the plain verb would (4.2.6): "you are allowed to eat". Probability is confidence (6.1).
- <span id="r-12-3"></span>Purpose is **fan** + verb, "in order to": **fan bika** "to buy".
- <span id="r-12-4"></span>"Let's" is **lo kan** …, and "don't" is **ti su** ….

## 13. Grammar words

<span id="r-13-1"></span>

| Group | Words |
|---|---|
| Speech acts | question **bi** · request **lo** · instruct **ti** · advise **fon** · intend **du** · warn **yo** · exclaim **ka** |
| Politeness | **wu** |
| Clause operator | confidence **pe** |
| Phrase operators | not **su** · only **ga** · also **pon** · even **yon** · focus **min** |
| Degree | more **ke** · very **ten** |
| Aspect | done **ben** · ongoing **li** |
| Sentence end | tag **bi** · "you know" **yan** |
| Pronouns | I **wan** · you **ba** · we incl. **kan** · we excl. **ta** · he/she **mon** · the other one **tun** · it **bun** · logophoric **son** · anyone **dan** · self **nen** |
| Determiners | plural **la** · this **yin** · that **fin** · all **we** · some **fe** · some-not-all **ye** · none **din** · unspecified **kin** · which/what **yu** |
| Relations | of **pi** · at **no** · to **len** · from/than **don** · with **nan** · for **fan** · before **ya** · after **kon** · like/about **fun** · during **pu** · because of **bo** |
| Linkers | and **nun** · or **lu** · but **fen** · if **sin** · because **bo** · that **me** · who/which **gi** · while **pu** · before **ya** · after **kon** |
| Copula | is **gun** |
| Noun phrase | modifier of a modifier **tan** · dear little **wi** |
| Names | **fo** |
| Derivation | doer **win** · thing **gen** · tool **to** · place **sen** |

<span id="r-13-2"></span>These operators have two syllables, stressed on the first: not literal **genso**, seen **wanme**, told **inwun**, inferred **wemen**, at least **konsin**, about to **buntu**, rather **tason**, almost **nedin**, each **mesun**, usually **manni**, "okay?" **buni**, "it turned out" **donon**, title **lanno**, until **anko**, without **nuwen**, end of phrase **kiyu**, number label **mena**. **mena** goes between a noun and a number to make a label or an ordinal (5.2). **anko** and **nuwen** are relation words, used like **ya** and **nan**: **wan doku anko lokagi** "I work until evening", **mon obu nuwen nasi** "he came without money". "Against" in the sense of protection is **don** "from" (**kuwi mon don fin** "protect him against that"); in the sense of opposing it is the verb **ninsu** "oppose".

## 14. Vocabulary

- <span id="r-14-1"></span>[../data/lexicon.tsv](../data/lexicon.tsv) lists roots and loans, each with a field and a frequency tier (1 rare – 5 most common). [../data/grammar.tsv](../data/grammar.tsv) lists the grammar words, [../data/numbers.tsv](../data/numbers.tsv) the number words and [../data/compounds.tsv](../data/compounds.tsv) the established compounds.
- <span id="r-14-2"></span>A gloss with a second gloss after a comma fixes the sense: **gufu** "hard, not soft". A root means that sense only.
- <span id="r-14-3"></span>One root may cover senses that most language families lump together: **tula** "do; make". Context tells them apart.

exec
/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc "sed -n '93,199p' docs/language.md" in /home/agent/work/repos/universal-language
 succeeded in 0ms:
- <span id="r-4-2-6"></span>When one verb follows another and the first takes an object (want, try, need, must, can, start, stop, teach, see, like, help…), the second verb is that object: **walo alun** "try to break". Otherwise the second verb describes the first: **doyu pume** "sit thinking", **wan isi yinmi tanke** "I go through the forest" (go passing the forest; there is no word for "through"). Phrases and clauses after a second verb that is an object belong to it, the nearest verb: **momu isi len guyi** "want to go home".
- <span id="r-4-2-7"></span>**tula** "do; make" followed by a result and then an object means "make (it) so": **tula upe yin** "fix this". Followed by an object and then a **fun** phrase saying how, it means "do (it) so": **tula yin fun upe** "do this well".
- <span id="r-4-2-8"></span>A command uses **ti**, or **lo** for a request. In casual speech a bare verb with no subject is also a command (**obu!** "come!"); **ti** is the precise form, and "don't" always needs it: **ti su kusa** "don't leave". Otherwise a sentence without a particle is a statement.

### 4.3 Openers

- <span id="r-4-3-6"></span>A linker is a word or phrase at the start that ties the sentence to the one before it, such as **bo yin** "so", **fen** "but" or **nun** "and also".
- <span id="r-4-3-1"></span>Any relation phrase may open the sentence, followed by a pause. A time or place phrase there sets the scene: **no minfi, …** "at noon, …". An opening phrase changes no roles.
- <span id="r-4-3-2"></span>A topic is **pi** + a noun phrase at the start, meaning "as for X": **pi tale la, win kuwi guyi, yidu bun la.** "As for the cars, the caretaker takes them." At the start, **pi** has no other reading, and the topic runs to its pause, or to **kiyu** "end of phrase", so it can be a list: **pi upe nun yomo,** "as for good and bad,".
- <span id="r-4-3-3"></span>An if/when clause is **sin** "if", **pu** "while/when", **kon** "after", **ya** "before" or **bo** "because", followed by a clause.
- <span id="r-4-3-4"></span>When several of these open a sentence, they come in this order: linker, scene or topic, if/when clause. The speech-act particle follows them.
- <span id="r-4-3-5"></span>Only a person word (4.1) calls someone. A longer noun phrase before a pause is the subject (4.6), and a fronted relation phrase or **pi** phrase is a scene or topic phrase.

### 4.4 Clauses and relation phrases

- <span id="r-4-4-1"></span>A relation phrase (**no**, **len**, **don**, **nan**, **fan**, **ya**, **kon**, **fun**, **pu**, **bo** "because of", and the two-syllable **anko** "until" and **nuwen** "without") at clause level attaches to the clause's verb. A relation phrase right after a noun may instead describe that noun: **osa nan tanun tufun** "tea with lemon" (not after a pronoun, and not **fun**, which after an object says how). To be precise, use a relative clause for the noun (**osa gi ibe tanun tufun**, **sola gi gun no fide pi yolan** "the road that is above the river") or put the phrase first for the verb (**nan tanun tufun, wan koye osa**). In comparisons and quantities (9.3), **don**, **len** and **fun** belong to their number or adjective. After a time, **kon** and **ya** belong to that time (8.2): **wan isi no wuma kon dili ni** "I go the day after tomorrow". **bo** with a noun phrase gives **bo yu** "why" and **bo yin** "so" (because of this).
- <span id="r-4-4-2"></span>A relation word may drop its object when context supplies it, as in **kon** "after that" and **ya** "beforehand". It then ends its clause or comes before a pause.
- <span id="r-4-4-3"></span>A relation word can take a clause (**kon mon la yidu ewo kin** "after they took someone") or an action noun (**don kusa** "from leaving").
- <span id="r-4-4-4"></span>A relative clause attaches to the nearest noun before it. A time, reason or condition clause at the *start* of a sentence belongs to the main clause. At the *end*, it belongs to the innermost clause before it, unless a pause closes that clause first (**kiyu** closes relative clauses but not a trailing clause like this one, 5.3): **pu leso, wan ben miya ewo gi sigo** "in the morning, I watched the person who came back"; **wan ben miya ewo gi sigo pu leso** "I watched the person who came back in the morning". In the same way, a phrase or clause after verbs joined by **nun**, **lu** or **fen** belongs to the last of them, and at the start it covers them all: **mon doku nun nawi no guyi** "he works, and eats at home"; **no guyi, mon doku nun nawi** "at home, he works and eats".
- <span id="r-4-4-5"></span>**nun** "and", **lu** "or" and **fen** "but" join nouns, verbs, modifiers or clauses. Nouns in a list are joined with **nun**: **mole nun sine** "mother and father". **lu X lu Y** means "either X or Y, not both".
- <span id="r-4-4-7"></span>Verbs joined by **nun**, **lu** or **fen** are separate predicates. Operators and aspect words before one of them cover only that verb, and an object after the last verb belongs to it: **mon su nawi nun fimo** "she doesn't eat, and she sleeps"; **wan nagu nun yidu bun** "I see (it) and take it".
- <span id="r-4-4-6"></span>A quote is framed by a say-verb, and in writing it goes in quotation marks. It is its own sentence with its own speech-act particle. Broken-off speech just stops.

### 4.5 Leaving things out

- <span id="r-4-5-1"></span>Anything the listener can supply from context may be left out: the subject, the object, the object of a relation word or the verb. An answer or follow-up can be just the new part: **nufi yu? osin?** "what time? again?"; **nagu ba la no dili mena ufo.** "see you all Saturday".
- <span id="r-4-5-2"></span>**gun** with no subject is "(it) is", like any verb with its subject left out: **gun nasi** "(it's) money". With **gun** left out too, a bare state is fine: **ten upe.** "very good." "There is" is **ibe** "have" with no subject (4.7).
- <span id="r-4-5-3"></span>When leaving something out could be unclear, say it.
- <span id="r-4-5-4"></span>An action word with no object is read by its subject. A subject that can't act undergoes the action: **beya ben tapi** "the door closed", **fileni ben alun** "the cup broke". A subject that can act does it to something the context supplies: **fo mara ben ibe fileni. mon ben alun.** "Mara had a cup. She broke it." To be precise, say the object, or use **nen** "self".

### 4.6 Pauses

<span id="r-4-6-1"></span>A pause, written as a comma, goes wherever a phrase could otherwise run on. It belongs in these places:
- <span id="r-4-6-2"></span>after someone called at the start, and before someone called at the end;
- <span id="r-4-6-3"></span>after an opener (linker, scene, topic, if/when clause);
- <span id="r-4-6-4"></span>after a subject that contains a clause or a phrasal derived word, before its verb (**win sanwa gi … ben pofi, kifa unfu …**);
- <span id="r-4-6-5"></span>between a recipient and the thing, where it helps: it is optional, and recommended when the thing is several words long or has a relative clause;
- <span id="r-4-6-6"></span>between clauses joined by **nun**, **lu** or **fen** when the first ends in a noun, and the same between joined verbs (4.4.7): **wan nawi pasu, nun fimo** "I eat food, and sleep";
- <span id="r-4-6-7"></span>before a stance word, and before the choice tag **yu** (4.8);
- <span id="r-4-6-8"></span>between a larger and a smaller calendar unit (**dunan mena yawu, dili mena tuli**);
- <span id="r-4-6-9"></span>after a relative clause, to close it before more material (or **kiyu**, 5.3).

<span id="r-4-6-10"></span>In casual writing, pauses that context makes obvious may be dropped.

### 4.7 "Is": gun

- <span id="r-4-7-1"></span>**gun** goes before a state: **guyi gun tugu** "the house is big". Without it, **guyi tugu** is normally read as "the big house".
- <span id="r-4-7-2"></span>**gun** goes before a noun: **mon gun tiwu** "she is a child".
- <span id="r-4-7-3"></span>**gun** goes before a place phrase: **mon gun no guyi** "he is at home".
- <span id="r-4-7-4"></span>**gun** before an action word gives the state of whoever undergoes the action: **gun tapi** "is closed", **mon gun kuwi** "she is protected", **wan gun nuta** "I am feared". This holds for every action word, feelings included; the one who feels takes the plain verb (**wan nuta** "I'm afraid").
- <span id="r-4-7-5"></span>**gun** before a **me** clause means "is that …".
- <span id="r-4-7-6"></span>**gun** with no subject is always "is" or "to be", with the subject understood: **momu gun tiwu** "want to be a child", **dan ibe sogan fan gun kuwi** "everyone has the right to be protected", **mon obu nun gun simi** "she came and is sick".
- <span id="r-4-7-7"></span>"There is" is **ibe** "have" with no subject, and **ibe** with no subject always means "there is": **ibe pasu** "there's food", **su ibe pasu** "there's no food", **fen, ibe yefi fefa** "but there are many things", **sin ibe pasu** "if there is food". To say someone has it, name them: **wan ibe pasu** "I have food".

### 4.8 Questions and answers

- <span id="r-4-8-1"></span>A yes/no question starts with **bi**. A **bi** at the end of a clause is a tag, "…, right?".
- <span id="r-4-8-2"></span>Question words stay where the answer goes and need no **bi**: **ba momu me wan oyi yu.** "you want me to say what?"
- <span id="r-4-8-3"></span>Question words are built from **yu**: **yu** what; **ewo yu** who; **X yu** which X; **no fela yu** where; **no nufi yu** when; **bo yu** why; **fun yu** how; **wifu yu** how many/much; **pi ewo yu** whose. **wifu yu** follows its noun the way a number does: **ewo wifu yu** "how many people".
- <span id="r-4-8-4"></span>To offer a choice, ask **bi … lu …** "either?", or ask **… lu …, yu** "which?" with a pause before **yu** so it isn't read as "which X". That second kind is answered by echoing the choice.
- <span id="r-4-8-5"></span>An indirect question starts its **me** clause with **bi**, with or without a question word: **wan su loya me bi mon obu** "I don't know whether she's coming", **wan su loya me bi ewo yu obu** "I don't know who is coming". Without **bi**, a question word anywhere in the sentence makes the whole sentence a question: **ba momu me wan oyi yu** "you want me to say what?"
- <span id="r-4-8-6"></span>Answers echo the verb, since there are no words for yes and no: "Aren't you coming?" → **obu** / **su obu**. A question with **gun** is answered by echoing what follows **gun**: "Is the house big?" → **tugu** / **su tugu**. The short answer is **feko** "true" or **su feko** "not true": it says whether the question's statement, exactly as asked, is true, so after a negative question **feko** agrees with the negative: **bi ba su obu** "aren't you coming?" → **feko** "right, I'm not". The echo is the precise answer. To acknowledge, **mm** says you heard, an echo says you understood, and **du** + verb says "will do". **ok** agrees to a proposal or instruction ("let's go" → **ok**) and doesn't answer a yes/no question.
- <span id="r-4-8-7"></span>A lost short word usually leaves a normal sentence, so nobody notices. When it matters, say it twice: **su su** for a "not" that must get through, a yes/no question ended with the tag **bi** as well (**bi ba obu, bi** "are you coming, are you?"), a warning repeated whole, and the answer echoed back in full (**wan obu** "I am coming"). In noise or across a distance, speak this way by default.

## 5. The noun phrase

### 5.1 Template

<span id="r-5-1-1"></span>A noun phrase runs in this order: noun, **wi** (5.2.12), content modifiers, number, **la**, determiner, **pi** phrase, relative clause, as in **guyi tugu sa la yin pi fo mara gi …**. Titles and **lanno** come before the noun (10.1), and phrase operators come before the whole phrase (6.2).

### 5.2 Parts

- <span id="r-5-2-1"></span>Describing words follow the noun: **guyi tugu** "big house". For a clear description, use **gi** (5.3).
- <span id="r-5-2-2"></span>Gender is a modifier, stated once when it matters: **tiwu futa** "daughter", **win wungu futa** "the leader, a woman".
- <span id="r-5-2-11"></span>Describing words after a noun each describe the noun: **wan ibe lipo wiwi buda** "I have a red, dark cloth". **tan** between two describing words makes the second describe the first: **wan ibe lipo wiwi tan buda** "I have a dark-red cloth". It joins only the word before it and the word after it, and works after **gun** too: **lipo gun wiwi tan buda** "the cloth is dark red".
- <span id="r-5-2-12"></span>**wi** right after a noun or a name adds affection, "dear little": **poka wi** "doggy", **fo mara wi** "dear Mara". It comes before the noun's describing words: **poka wi tugu** "the dear big dog". As a grammar word it also ends a name, so a content word can follow it with no pause: **fo mara wi obu** "dear Mara is coming".
- <span id="r-5-2-3"></span>Noun + noun is used only for gender and for the compounds in `compounds.tsv`. Other relations use **pi** or a relative clause.
- <span id="r-5-2-4"></span>Numbers and times can stand alone as noun phrases: **yawu gun upe** "ten is fine", **wuma gun upe** "tomorrow is fine".
- <span id="r-5-2-5"></span>A noun followed by a number is a count (**dili oma** "seven days"). A noun + **mena** + number is a label (**pata mena sa** "document 2"), and a label also gives a place in order: **bifo mena de** "the third time", **ewo mena ni** "the first person". "Last" is **pi fugi**, "of the end": **bifo pi fugi** "the last time". With a number before **pi** "of" and a noun after it, the phrase is a part of a set: **sa pi ewo de** "two of the three people". Two numbers joined by **pi** are a fraction (9.2.7).
- <span id="r-5-2-6"></span>The plural word **la** is optional. A count or context can show plurality.
- <span id="r-5-2-7"></span>There are no articles. Whether something is known or new is left to context, or shown with **yin**, **fin**, **kin** or a number.
- <span id="r-5-2-8"></span>Determiners (table in section 13) and **mesun** "each" follow the noun: **guyi yin** "this house", **ewo kin** "someone", **yefi kin** "something", **sen nume mesun** "each home". A determiner right after a noun or pronoun belongs to it. So when the thing given is a pronoun or a determiner standing alone, mark the recipient with **len**, the normal form there: **wan iko fin len mon** "I told that to him" (**wan iko mon fin** is "I told that-him"). A determiner may stand alone as a noun phrase: **we** "all", **fe** "some", **din** "none". A part of a whole is a determiner or number + **pi** + noun: **ye pi silu la** "some of the photos".
- <span id="r-5-2-9"></span>**pi** "of" links a noun to a noun: **guyi pi fo mara** "Mara's house". Each **pi** attaches to the nearest noun before it, and its phrase is the nearest single noun after it, so a list joined by **nun** or **lu** is outside it: **enpa pi pume nun lite pi nubige** is "freedom of thought, and religion". To cover every item, repeat the head: **enpa pi pume nun enpa pi lite pi nubige** "freedom of thought and freedom of religion".
- <span id="r-5-2-10"></span>"One's own" is **pi nen**: **wan ibe guyi pi nen** "I have my own house". Since a **pi** phrase holds a single noun, two owners of one thing take a relative clause: **guyi gi fo mara nun wan ibe** "Mara's and my house".

### 5.3 Relative clauses

<span id="r-5-3-1"></span>A relative clause is **gi** + clause, placed after the noun. The noun fills the role missing from the clause:
- <span id="r-5-3-2"></span>subject: **ewo gi tedo** "the person who walks";
- <span id="r-5-3-3"></span>object: **natun gi ba la yaga** "the papers you put";
- <span id="r-5-3-4"></span>place or time, with the relation word at the end of the clause: **fela gi wan nume no** "the place where I live". The precise form keeps a pronoun after the relation word: **fela gi wan nume no bun** "the place I live in (it)";
- <span id="r-5-3-6"></span>any other relation, the same way: **wonnun gi wan fonsu nan** "the tool I cut with";
- <span id="r-5-3-7"></span>"whose", with **pi** + the pronoun that fits the noun (**pi mon**, **pi bun**, **pi mon la**) inside the clause pointing back to it: **ewo gi guyi pi mon gun tugu** "the person whose house is big", **guyi gi beya pi bun gun tapi** "the house whose door is closed".

<span id="r-5-3-8"></span>If both the subject and the object are missing, the noun is the subject: **ewo gi nagu** "the person who sees". For "the person I see", keep the subject: **ewo gi wan nagu**.

<span id="r-5-3-5"></span>A relative clause attaches to the nearest noun or pronoun before it, so after a **pi** phrase it describes the **pi** noun. To describe the head instead, put a quality before the **pi** phrase or use a new sentence. A pause, or the word **kiyu** "end of phrase", closes it, along with every relative clause still open: **wan ben miya ewo gi sigo kiyu pu leso** "I watched the person who came back, in the morning". **kiyu** is optional; use it where a missed pause could mislead. It closes only the phrases that run on: relative clauses, phrasal derived words (5.4) and topics (4.3). A trailing clause (4.4.4) is closed by a pause. Because one **kiyu** closes every open relative clause, a phrase can't be attached to an outer relative clause while an inner one is open; the precise way is to restructure, for example with a second sentence.

### 5.4 Derived words

<span id="r-5-4-1"></span>Four markers before a verb or verb phrase name a role in the action:

<span id="r-5-4-2"></span>

| Marker | Role | Example |
|---|---|---|
| **win** | the one who does it | **win febo** "one who answers" |

