| Writing: notation passes through, ? and ! allowed, capitals harmless | Text and computer use; usage bans became predictions (Restraint) | — |
| Commas droppable in chat | Comma test: 0 of 67 lines lost meaning without them; a precise way must exist, not always be used [author] | Targeted test if names at the end or topic openers get misread |

**Clear forms by meaning, not word kind** [author, 2026-10-11]. The last row of the interpersonal table here first became a rule (each root has a default kind; a quality after a noun describes it, an action after a subject is its verb), then a convention. Under the priority order it failed predictability: about 50 borderline words had an arbitrary kind. Now the speaker picks the form by what they mean: **gun** for a state, an aspect word for an event, **gi** for a description. It works for any word, with nothing to memorise. Bare noun + word is left to context. Mandarin works the same way (很, 了/在, 的). The `kind` column was removed; add it back as a dictionary hint if learners keep asking how a word is typically used.

## 11. Known weaknesses (what a successor should doubt first)

1. **Validated mostly by its own instruments:** the confusion model is scored against itself; blind readers are AI agents with world knowledge. One human has read it aloud; none has listened.
2. **English-centred:** listener data, test texts, readers, English glosses (ambiguous ones pinned with a second gloss since 2026-10-10), technical loans. "Universal" is a goal, not a result.
3. **The full one-syllable space:** every future grammar word costs two syllables.
4. **The watch list** (drift.md): confidence collapsing or used for emphasis, politeness taking over hedges, topic-based pronouns needing a mechanical fallback, aspect words unused.
5. **Frequency tiers and lump/split thresholds are judgement**, not data.

