docs/language.md:4:<span id="r-0-1"></span>This document states what the language is. The reasons for each part are in [rationale.md](rationale.md), its history is in [design-log.md](design-log.md), and the word lists are in the [data folder](https://github.com/LilijoySkyseeker/apupiwe/tree/main/data).
docs/language.md:6:<span id="r-0-2"></span>The language calls itself **apu pi we** "everyone's language", said [ˈapu pi we], roughly AH-poo pee weh, with only the first syllable stressed. Its name in English and other languages is one word, Apupiwe, said the same way. Any language is named with **apu pi** followed by whose language it is.
docs/language.md:8:<span id="r-0-3"></span>Apupiwe is a designed language for people who speak it natively, within the world's existing cultures. Its own grammar is built for native speakers. Where it meets a culture's conventions, such as clocks, calendars, units, names and customs, it carries them. Where conventions conflict and one must be chosen, it follows the international standard.
docs/language.md:16:1. <span id="r-1-1"></span>The common case costs nothing. Statements, ordinary belief, an unknown source, the topic, and present or unspecified time are unmarked. Frequent meanings have the shortest words.
docs/language.md:17:2. <span id="r-1-2"></span>Heads come first, and position carries role. The order is subject, verb, object. A content word that describes a noun or a verb follows it. Words never move to show their role.
docs/language.md:18:3. <span id="r-1-3"></span>Operators come before what they cover (section 6).
docs/language.md:19:4. <span id="r-1-4"></span>Heavy goes last. A noun's own modifiers come before its **pi** "of" phrase, and long clauses go to the end. Nothing may follow a relative clause in its phrase.
docs/language.md:27:- <span id="r-2-2"></span>There are 13 consonants: p b t d k g · m n · f s · l w y. They are made at the lips (p b m w, and f with lip and teeth), with the tongue tip (t d n s l), with the tongue body (y) and with the back of the tongue (k g). In the reference pronunciation p t k are aspirated at the start of a word, with a puff of air as in English *pin*, and b d g are voiced, so the pairs differ both ways: speakers of languages that tell them apart by voicing and those that tell them apart by aspiration both hear the difference. Unaspirated p t k are a harmless variant.
docs/language.md:37:- <span id="r-2-9"></span>These accent variants are harmless: they never turn one word into another. p t k with or without the puff of air; b d g softened between vowels in an unstressed syllable, as in Spanish *cada*; k and g sounding like "ch" and "j" before i and e; l dark, or as a tap as in Spanish *pero*; f made with both lips; w made with the lip and teeth, as in Dutch *wat*; s as "sh"; a vowel plus n as a nasal vowel; and a catch in the throat (a glottal stop) where a word ending in a vowel meets one starting with a vowel. The reference pronunciation uses the plain sounds, keeps every vowel full, and doesn't lengthen stressed syllables, but none of that is a rule.
docs/language.md:40:- <span id="r-2-12"></span>No two content words, and no two short words (grammar words and digits) that can stand in the same place, differ only by sounds a harmless variant (2.9) brings together: b and w, d and l, and g and w before o and u, where softening applies (between vowels, in an unstressed syllable: a one-syllable grammar word, or a later syllable of a longer word); g and y, or k and s, before i and e, anywhere. So a speaker with any of these accents is understood, and no contrast hangs on the sound changes these variants begin. A new grammar word also never differs only by its vowel from a grammar word that can stand in its place, since unstressed vowels weaken.
docs/language.md:44:- <span id="r-3-1"></span>The letters are a b d e f g i k l m n o p s t u w y, one per sound, with no accents. Digits never appear inside a word. Spelling follows the standard pronunciation, and neither the harmless variants nor stress are written.
docs/language.md:52:  | `" "` | around quoted speech |
docs/language.md:55:- <span id="r-3-4"></span>Some things pass through as written: chat abbreviations and interjections (ok, lol), digits, unit symbols (`5 km`, `20 °C`, `30%`), the local clock notation (`21:30`, `9:30 pm`), ISO dates (`2026-10-10`), letters used as labels, links, email addresses, @handles, #tags, emoji, code, file names, and personal and brand names (section 11). Unit symbols are read aloud by their borrowed names (9.3) and clock notation in the speaker's clock style (8.3). Other notation is read as the speaker's culture reads it.
docs/language.md:71:| 3 | Speech act | one particle; **genso** or **ka** may come before it | 7.1 |
docs/language.md:100:- <span id="r-4-3-1"></span>Any relation phrase may open the sentence, followed by a pause. A time or place phrase there sets the scene: **no minfi, …** "at noon, …". An opening phrase changes no roles.
docs/language.md:103:- <span id="r-4-3-4"></span>When several of these open a sentence, they come in this order: linker, scene or topic, if/when clause. The speech-act particle follows them.
docs/language.md:108:- <span id="r-4-4-1"></span>A relation phrase (**no**, **len**, **don**, **nan**, **fan**, **ya**, **kon**, **fun**, **pu**, **bo** "because of", and the two-syllable **anko** "until" and **nuwen** "without") at clause level attaches to the clause's verb. A relation phrase right after a noun may instead describe that noun: **osa nan tanun tufun** "tea with lemon" (not after a pronoun, and not **fun**, which after an object says how). To be precise, use a relative clause for the noun (**osa gi ibe tanun tufun**, **sola gi gun no fide pi yolan** "the road that is above the river") or put the phrase first for the verb (**nan tanun tufun, wan koye osa**). In comparisons and quantities (9.3), **don**, **len** and **fun** belong to their number or adjective. After a time, **kon** and **ya** belong to that time (8.2): **wan isi no wuma kon dili ni** "I go the day after tomorrow". **bo** with a noun phrase gives **bo yu** "why" and **bo yin** "so" (because of this).
docs/language.md:111:- <span id="r-4-4-4"></span>A relative clause attaches to the nearest noun before it. A time, reason or condition clause at the *start* of a sentence belongs to the main clause. At the *end*, it belongs to the innermost clause before it, unless a pause closes that clause first (**kiyu** closes relative clauses but not a trailing clause like this one, 5.3): **pu leso, wan ben miya ewo gi sigo** "in the morning, I watched the person who came back"; **wan ben miya ewo gi sigo pu leso** "I watched the person who came back in the morning". In the same way, a phrase or clause after verbs joined by **nun**, **lu** or **fen** belongs to the last of them, and at the start it covers them all: **mon doku nun nawi no guyi** "he works, and eats at home"; **no guyi, mon doku nun nawi** "at home, he works and eats".
docs/language.md:114:- <span id="r-4-4-6"></span>A quote is framed by a say-verb, and in writing it goes in quotation marks. It is its own sentence with its own speech-act particle. Broken-off speech just stops.
docs/language.md:130:- <span id="r-4-6-6"></span>between clauses joined by **nun**, **lu** or **fen** when the first ends in a noun, and the same between joined verbs (4.4.7): **wan nawi pasu, nun fimo** "I eat food, and sleep";
docs/language.md:132:- <span id="r-4-6-8"></span>between a larger and a smaller calendar unit (**dunan mena yawu, dili mena tuli**);
docs/language.md:144:- <span id="r-4-7-6"></span>**gun** with no subject is always "is" or "to be", with the subject understood: **momu gun tiwu** "want to be a child", **dan ibe sogan fan gun kuwi** "everyone has the right to be protected", **mon obu nun gun simi** "she came and is sick".
docs/language.md:145:- <span id="r-4-7-7"></span>"There is" is **ibe** "have" with no subject, and **ibe** with no subject always means "there is": **ibe pasu** "there's food", **su ibe pasu** "there's no food", **fen, ibe yefi fefa** "but there are many things", **sin ibe pasu** "if there is food". To say someone has it, name them: **wan ibe pasu** "I have food".
docs/language.md:150:- <span id="r-4-8-2"></span>Question words stay where the answer goes and need no **bi**: **ba momu me wan oyi yu.** "you want me to say what?"
docs/language.md:153:- <span id="r-4-8-5"></span>An indirect question starts its **me** clause with **bi**, with or without a question word: **wan su loya me bi mon obu** "I don't know whether she's coming", **wan su loya me bi ewo yu obu** "I don't know who is coming". Without **bi**, a question word anywhere in the sentence makes the whole sentence a question: **ba momu me wan oyi yu** "you want me to say what?"
docs/language.md:154:- <span id="r-4-8-6"></span>Answers echo the verb, since there are no words for yes and no: "Aren't you coming?" → **obu** / **su obu**. A question with **gun** is answered by echoing what follows **gun**: "Is the house big?" → **tugu** / **su tugu**. The short answer is **feko** "true" or **su feko** "not true": it says whether the question's statement, exactly as asked, is true, so after a negative question **feko** agrees with the negative: **bi ba su obu** "aren't you coming?" → **feko** "right, I'm not". The echo is the precise answer. To acknowledge, **mm** says you heard, an echo says you understood, and **du** + verb says "will do". **ok** agrees to a proposal or instruction ("let's go" → **ok**) and doesn't answer a yes/no question.
docs/language.md:161:<span id="r-5-1-1"></span>A noun phrase runs in this order: noun, **wi** (5.2.12), content modifiers, number, **la**, determiner, **pi** phrase, relative clause, as in **guyi tugu sa la yin pi fo mara gi …**. Titles and **lanno** come before the noun (10.1), and phrase operators come before the whole phrase (6.2).
docs/language.md:168:- <span id="r-5-2-12"></span>**wi** right after a noun or a name adds affection, "dear little": **poka wi** "doggy", **fo mara wi** "dear Mara". It comes before the noun's describing words: **poka wi tugu** "the dear big dog". As a grammar word it also ends a name, so a content word can follow it with no pause: **fo mara wi obu** "dear Mara is coming".
docs/language.md:174:- <span id="r-5-2-8"></span>Determiners (table in section 13) and **mesun** "each" follow the noun: **guyi yin** "this house", **ewo kin** "someone", **yefi kin** "something", **sen nume mesun** "each home". A determiner right after a noun or pronoun belongs to it. So when the thing given is a pronoun or a determiner standing alone, mark the recipient with **len**, the normal form there: **wan iko fin len mon** "I told that to him" (**wan iko mon fin** is "I told that-him"). A determiner may stand alone as a noun phrase: **we** "all", **fe** "some", **din** "none". A part of a whole is a determiner or number + **pi** + noun: **ye pi silu la** "some of the photos".
docs/language.md:175:- <span id="r-5-2-9"></span>**pi** "of" links a noun to a noun: **guyi pi fo mara** "Mara's house". Each **pi** attaches to the nearest noun before it, and its phrase is the nearest single noun after it, so a list joined by **nun** or **lu** is outside it: **enpa pi pume nun lite pi nubige** is "freedom of thought, and religion". To cover every item, repeat the head: **enpa pi pume nun enpa pi lite pi nubige** "freedom of thought and freedom of religion".
docs/language.md:183:- <span id="r-5-3-4"></span>place or time, with the relation word at the end of the clause: **fela gi wan nume no** "the place where I live". The precise form keeps a pronoun after the relation word: **fela gi wan nume no bun** "the place I live in (it)";
docs/language.md:184:- <span id="r-5-3-6"></span>any other relation, the same way: **wonnun gi wan fonsu nan** "the tool I cut with";
docs/language.md:189:<span id="r-5-3-5"></span>A relative clause attaches to the nearest noun or pronoun before it, so after a **pi** phrase it describes the **pi** noun. To describe the head instead, put a quality before the **pi** phrase or use a new sentence. A pause, or the word **kiyu** "end of phrase", closes it, along with every relative clause still open: **wan ben miya ewo gi sigo kiyu pu leso** "I watched the person who came back, in the morning". **kiyu** is optional; use it where a missed pause could mislead. It closes only the phrases that run on: relative clauses, phrasal derived words (5.4) and topics (4.3). A trailing clause (4.4.4) is closed by a pause. Because one **kiyu** closes every open relative clause, a phrase can't be attached to an outer relative clause while an inner one is open; the precise way is to restructure, for example with a second sentence.
docs/language.md:193:<span id="r-5-4-1"></span>Four markers before a verb or verb phrase name a role in the action:
docs/language.md:205:- <span id="r-5-4-4"></span>The base may be a verb phrase: **win isi don guyi** "one who goes from home", a traveller. It runs to the end of its phrase, like a relative clause, and a pause or **kiyu** separates it from a following verb or describing word: **to siten sokun** "a pan" (a thing for cooking flat), **to siten sokun kiyu tikun** "a hot pan"; without **kiyu**, **tikun** would join the phrase inside. It also ends before a name (**fo** …) or a gender word (**futa**, **lowu**), so **win wiwa fo mara** is "Teacher Mara" and **win wungu futa** "the leader, a woman".
docs/language.md:226:- <span id="r-6-2-6"></span>degree words **ke** more, **ten** very, **tason** rather (**ke tugu** "bigger"; **nasi ke uyo** "less money").
docs/language.md:238:| **lo** | request; with another subject, a wish (**lo wan nagu** "let me see"; **lo nubige kuwi ba** "may God keep you") |
docs/language.md:248:- <span id="r-7-1-4"></span>Someone else's intention is reported (**inwun**) or stated with time words and **pe**; in the reference variety, **du** is for the speaker's own commitment.
docs/language.md:257:- <span id="r-7-2-6"></span>A half-remembered "wasn't it?" is low confidence plus the tag: **pe de mon ben oyi bun, bi.** "she said it, I think, didn't she?"
docs/language.md:265:- <span id="r-8-1-1"></span>Tense is optional. A time word or the first **ben** of a narrative sets the frame, the time that what follows is about, and later verbs in that frame stay unmarked. The future is shown by a time word or by context: **no wuma, gegi nuwo** "it will rain tomorrow".
docs/language.md:266:- <span id="r-8-1-2"></span>Aspect words come right before the verb. **ben** means done. With no frame set it reads as past. Inside a past frame it marks something already done by that time, "had done", and inside a future frame it is "will have". **li** means ongoing, and also "still". **buntu** means about to. **ben li** is "was doing, had been doing": ongoing before now, or before the time being talked about (**wan ben li nawi** "I was eating").
docs/language.md:268:- <span id="r-8-1-4"></span>**manni** "usually" marks habit. With a past frame it means "used to".
docs/language.md:271:### 8.2 Time words
docs/language.md:273:- <span id="r-8-2-1"></span>The time roots are **eda** now, **mefa** today, **wuma** tomorrow, **afe** yesterday; **dili** day, **nelon** week (7 days), **dunan** month, **fawu** year; **nufi** time (a span or a point); **bifo** occasion (**bifo sa** "twice").
docs/language.md:274:- <span id="r-8-2-2"></span>A time can be an anchor plus an offset, and the offset names its unit: **wuma kon dili ni** "the day after tomorrow". With no anchor, the offset counts from the current frame (8.1), which is now unless a story has set another time: **kon nelon ni** "a week from now" (seven days on; the calendar's next week is **nelon lolo**), **ya dunan ni** "a month ago". Any time or event can be the anchor.
docs/language.md:275:- <span id="r-8-2-3"></span>A duration is a count: **yiwo de** "three hours". **pu** + duration means "during, for" a span that frames the clause: **pu dili oma** "for seven days".
docs/language.md:276:- <span id="r-8-2-4"></span>Age is lived time: **wan nume fawu de yawu** "I have lived 30 years".
docs/language.md:279:### 8.3 Clock, calendar, dates
docs/language.md:287:- <span id="r-8-3-3"></span>Dates are labels (5.2) and put the larger unit first, with a pause between: **dunan mena yawu, dili mena tuli** "October 9". A weekday with a time is said the same way: **dili mena ma, no …**. Years are read digit by digit: **fawu mena sa mu sa ufo** "2026".
docs/language.md:307:- <span id="r-9-2-1"></span>The place words are **yawu** 10, **loki** 100, **munno** 1000, **tiya** million and **siwin** billion. The last three are also group words. In the quantity reading, place words come in descending order, each preceded by its digit. A 1 before a place word is dropped, and zero places are skipped: 15 **yawu ma**, 50 **ma yawu**, 42 **wo yawu sa**, 105 **loki ma**. A group word takes a whole number before it: 250 000 = **sa loki ma yawu munno**.
docs/language.md:309:- <span id="r-9-2-3"></span>Labels (noun + **mena** + number) under 100 use the quantity reading (**dili mena yawu wo** "day 14"). Larger labels use the digit reading.
docs/language.md:311:- <span id="r-9-2-5"></span>Arithmetic aloud is a reading of notation, like spelling aloud, not an ordinary sentence: read the signs in the order written, + **ginni**, − **guti**, × **bifo**, ÷ **gonwu**, = **gun**: **sa ginni de gun ma** "2 + 3 = 5", **de bifo wo gun yawu sa** "3 × 4 = 12", **ufo gonwu sa gun de** "6 ÷ 2 = 3". **guti** before a number makes it negative and between two numbers means minus; a list keeps its pauses (**ma, guti de** "5, −3").
docs/language.md:328:- <span id="r-10-1-2"></span>Titles go before the name with no comma, and work both in address and in reference: **win wiwa fo mara** "Teacher Mara"; **win wiwa fo mara ben oyi** "Teacher Mara said".
docs/language.md:345:- <span id="r-10-3-4"></span>**son**, in reported speech or thought, is the person whose words are reported.
docs/language.md:347:- <span id="r-10-3-6"></span>**nen** is self. As an object it is reflexive (**wan kuwi nen** "I keep myself"). After a noun or pronoun it means "in person" (**mon nen obu** "he came himself").
docs/language.md:349:- <span id="r-10-3-8"></span>A pronoun never means the subject of its own clause; for that, use **nen**. The listener finds referents from context. If one could be unclear, name it.
docs/language.md:353:- <span id="r-11-1"></span>Names take **fo**: **fo mara**. That is the precise form; casual speech often leaves **fo** out when a name can't be taken for a word (**mara ben obu** "Mara came"). A personal or brand name keeps its owner's spelling in lowercase, even with letters the language lacks: **fo denise**, **fo xi jinping**, **fo nguyen**, **fo wi-fi**. Say it as close to its owner's pronunciation as you can. Place names are names too: **fo london**, though an established adapted form may be used instead: **fo landan**. Names aren't used as verbs. A name runs until the next grammar word or pause, so a name of several words is written with no grammar words inside it, and a content word right after a name needs a grammar word or a pause before it: **fo xi jinping ben oyi** "Xi Jinping said". A name that contains a grammar word is closed with **kiyu** instead, and then runs until it: **fo cuerpo de paz kiyu ben obu** "the Peace Corps came". **kiyu** closes an open relative clause, built word or topic first, and a name only when none of those is open, so in **guyi gi fo mara ben miya kiyu gun tugu** "the house that Mara saw is big" it closes the clause and the name is **mara**. A name that needs **kiyu** therefore can't stand inside a relative clause; say it first, as a topic, or in its own sentence.
docs/language.md:354:- <span id="r-11-2"></span>The adapted form (`tools/names.py`) is the spoken fallback for a name and the form of every loan. It is made like this:
docs/language.md:355:  - each foreign sound becomes the nearest native sound: r → l, v → b, ʋ → w, z/sh/zh → s, th → f, dh → d, ch → t and j → d (but k and g before i or e, whose harmless variant is the original sound: *gene* **geni**), kh → k; h and glottal sounds disappear; ng → n; length, aspiration and tone are dropped; ə, ʌ, æ → a;
docs/language.md:359:- <span id="r-11-3"></span>The language's own vocabulary borrows only where the world shares a form, taken in that international form: **dolalu** dollar, **kilometulu**, **intelunetu**, **imeyilu**, **kamela**. A loan enters the vocabulary once it is common, and until then near-collisions are tolerated. A loan is identified by its consonants: the vowels added to fit the syllables (the final **u** of **kodu** "code") and the **l** that stands for an r may be said as in the speaker's own form of the loan, and no other word sits where such a variant would land. Speakers also borrow freely from their own languages, as speakers of every language do. A borrowed word takes no **fo** and is used like any word. Say it in its own sounds or the language's, and write it by its sound in the language's letters, as in the adapted form (11.2): **wan nawi susi sa** "I ate two sushi". Casual writing often keeps the source spelling at first (*sushi*); borrowings settle into the language's letters as they're used. A borrowed word can do anything a word can, including be a verb (**wan ben gugalu bun** "I googled it"), while a name with **fo** is never a verb (**wan ben ditun bun no fo google** "I searched for it on Google"). A borrowed word that would sound like a word of the language is reshaped: adapted another way, or replaced by a native word or phrase (**fo** marks only names, which are never verbs). Borrowings that catch on drift toward the language's sounds; `tools/names.py` gives the written form. Native words, built words and phrases can always say the same thing.
docs/language.md:362:- To spell aloud for someone of another culture (a name, an email address, a code), say each letter by its word from the international spelling alphabet (ICAO), adapted to this language's sounds: a **alufa**, b **bulabo**, c **saluli**, d **deluta**, e **eko**, f **fokusutulotu**, g **golufu**, h **otelu**, i **indiya**, j **duliyetu**, k **kilo**, l **lima**, m **mayiku**, n **nobenbalu**, o **osuka**, p **papa**, q **kebeku**, r **lomeyo**, s **siyela**, t **tango**, u **yunifolumu**, v **bikuta**, w **wisuki**, x **ekusuleyi**, y **yanki**, z **sulu**. Within one culture its own letter names are fine. **kilo** is also "kilogram" (9.3.5); in spelling it is always a letter, as in the original alphabet.
docs/language.md:387:| Determiners | plural **la** · this **yin** · that **fin** · all **we** · some **fe** · some-not-all **ye** · none **din** · unspecified **kin** · which/what **yu** |
docs/language.md:395:<span id="r-13-2"></span>These operators have two syllables, stressed on the first: not literal **genso**, seen **wanme**, told **inwun**, inferred **wemen**, at least **konsin**, about to **buntu**, rather **tason**, almost **nedin**, each **mesun**, usually **manni**, "okay?" **buni**, "it turned out" **donon**, title **lanno**, until **anko**, without **nuwen**, end of phrase **kiyu**, number label **mena**. **mena** goes between a noun and a number to make a label or an ordinal (5.2). **anko** and **nuwen** are relation words, used like **ya** and **nan**: **wan doku anko lokagi** "I work until evening", **mon obu nuwen nasi** "he came without money". "Against" in the sense of protection is **don** "from" (**kuwi mon don fin** "protect him against that"); in the sense of opposing it is the verb **ninsu** "oppose".

