- ~~**No two grammar words differ only by a final -n**~~ retired 2026-10-16 [author]: replaced by the position rule (no such pair among words that share a position) plus model v2's direct scoring of final-n loss (design log).

### 3.4 Juncture (`tools/juncture.py`)
[Claude, idea from senary] Written tests can't hear word boundaries. Rules: no content word begins with a one-syllable digit's syllable (so "digit + grammar word" can't sound like a content word); no grammar word ending in -n before a vowel-initial word that would be heard as another word. (-n pairs: only in a shared position, §3.3, since 2026-11-19.) Loans are exempt from the digit rule (their forms come from the world); `KNOWN` in the tool records any accepted case (none since gene became **geni**, 2026-11-19). Run after any addition.

### 3.4a Accent tolerance (`tools/accent.py`; language.md 2.9, 2.12)
[Author, 2026-11-19; `tests/accent-01/`, `tests/shortwords-04/`] Speakers live among other languages and bring their accents, and children copy them (P4, B9). So the reference declares which realisations count as the same sound (2.9) and builds the words so that none of them turns one word into another (2.12). The cold review's replacement for "grammar words keep their consonants", a norm speakers can't obey: no contrast between words that can share a position may rest on a declared variant. The variants that mattered are the ones speech produces first: voiced stops softening between vowels, and k g palatalised before front vowels. Short, unstressed, frequent words wear fastest, so the grammar words were re-formed under the rule (27 changed), and digits, the name and the 22 grammar words that often precede roots constrain that (haplology). Vowel-only differences between same-position grammar words are kept out of new grammar words, since unstressed vowels weaken. Run `tools/accent.py` after any word change.

### 3.5 The optimiser (`tests/placement-05/place.py`; `tools/words.py add-content` is out of date)
**Since 2026-11-18 roots are placed by the placement tool** (`tests/placement-01` to `-05`): model v2's robust score over 48 listener variants, the length rule (three syllables only for the rarest tier), the haplology, digit, juncture and accent rules, and a keep term so placed roots stay. `words.py add-content` below still uses model v1 and lacks those rules (roadmap); use the placement tool until it is updated.
| 4. Heavy goes last | [evidence] processing (end weight) in head-initial languages like this one; head-final languages prefer long before short (Hawkins 1994; Yamashita & Chang 2001) *(corrected 2026-10-12 after the fact-check, C16: not a universal)* | Relative clauses have no end marker, so nothing may follow them in their phrase. |
| 5. Doubling widens or strengthens | [Claude] | A built-in renewal path: when a word wears down, its double takes over (**su su**, **wu wu**) inside the system. |
| 6. Every social pressure gets its own outlet | [evidence] drift items 1–2 | Emphasis (**ka**, **ten**) and politeness (**wu**) have their own words so they don't wear down the precision words. Since the Restraint review this is a design plus a *prediction*, not a usage ban. |

### 4.2 The grammar-word layer
- **One syllable, unstressed, 64 words; the space is full** (with 13 consonants, 5 vowels, ±n, minus digits, same-slot and -n rules). New grammar words must be two syllables. This is why digits 6–9 are two syllables.
- **Why particles, not inflection:** [evidence, thin, + Claude] creoles are mostly analytic (not uniformly: Tok Pisin *-im*, Saramaccan tone and morphology); particles are what drift turns into affixes anyway. Fusion of particles later is harmless because same-slot words differ by consonant. *(Corrected 2026-10-12 after the fact-check, C17, C18. Was "native speakers of designed languages drop redundant mandatory morphology (Bergen 2001)": Bergen's 8 children dropped accusatives in step with their other native language, i.e. transfer, not nativisation. Cut as wrong: "isolating languages are where all successful auxlangs and creoles converged": Esperanto, the one successful auxlang, is agglutinative.)*

### 4.3 Speech acts, confidence, source
- **Speech-act particle first** (**bi lo ti fon du yo genso ka**) [evidence + Claude, research round 1]: hinted and mitigated speech is a documented safety problem (Fischer & Orasanu 1999; Tenerife 1977; Avianca 52, whose crew never said "emergency"), which supports marking act and urgency in words. That melody alone fails in noise and across cultures is our extrapolation: no study shows it, or that a marker prevents it. Initial position tells the listener the sentence's shape early (like Spanish ¿) [Claude]; for question particles it is the minority position (WALS 92: 129 initial vs 314 final). *(Corrected 2026-10-12 after the fact-check, C19.)*
- **Confidence as a number** (**pe** + 0–6, sixths) [evidence + author]: number-anchored probability was the strongest of the tested fixes for miscommunication of stance (words like "likely" span huge ranges). Friedman et al. 2018 (888,328 forecasts): rounding to seven bins cost untrained forecasters little (mean Brier 0.5% worse, median 0.2%, not significant) but cost superforecasters 40%, and the paper concludes that coarsening "consistently sacrifices predictive accuracy". So it doesn't favour sixths over tenths; it says coarse steps cost ordinary speakers little, and skilled users need finer ones (half-steps, percentages). The author switched to tenths on 2026-10-09 for familiarity and back to sixths on 2026-10-10: they "fit the research" (that rested on the misread citation), almost every value is one syllable (the reason that stands), and exact percentages remain sayable as a number out of a hundred (**pi loki**). *(Corrected 2026-10-12 after the fact-check, C21: was "Sixths match the ~7 bands forecasters can use".)* Optional. Watch list: collapsing to favourite values.
- **Source (evidentiality) optional** (**wanme inwun wemen**) [evidence]: the case for obligatory evidentials was mixed to negative (Tosun, Vaid & Geraci 2013). Láadan made them obligatory and failed, but Elgin put that down to lack of uptake, so it says little about evidentials *(corrected 2026-10-12 after the fact-check, C22)*. Every marked dimension has an "unspecified" value (**kin**) so nothing forces false precision.
- **No yes/no; echo answers** [evidence]: answers to negative questions are a documented failure ("yes" means opposite things across languages). Acknowledgement ladder from aviation: **mm** heard, echo understood, **du** will do.

- **Each speech-act word's source** (author, 2026-10-13; answers the review's point that they rested on one philosopher's taxonomy): **bi**, a question that tone alone carries in 18% of languages and loses in writing and noise, while 61% use such a particle (WALS 116); **ti** vs **fon**, instruction vs advice, the authority distinction behind cockpit accidents (Tenerife), and **fon** also carries "should"; **lo**, conventionally indirect requests read differently across cultures (CCSARP); **wu**, politeness kept apart from doubt (Bonnefon & Villejoubert 2006); **yo** and **yo yo**, urgency signalled too softly (Avianca 52; MAYDAY / PAN-PAN), rare but catastrophic; **genso**, sarcasm in writing read at 56%, barely above chance, against 73% by voice (Kruger et al. 2005); **ka**, surprise or strong feeling for the whole utterance, which tone carries in speech and only "!" in writing (B4: no meaning rests on punctuation, the same reason as **bi** and **genso**), and one shared exclamation word instead of each culture's interjections (B1); earlier sources: drift item 1 (emphasis gets its own outlet so **pe** doesn't bleach) and the grammar rebuild, which protected **ka** despite low measured use (design log, "Protected despite low measurements"); **du**, used naturally for "I'll …" and on the common path from intention to future; the sentence-end word **yan** ("you see", shared ground), common across languages (Japanese *ne*/*yo*, Mandarin *ba*, Tagalog *naman*), to be given a consonant-initial form in the short-word rebuild.

### 4.4 Reference
- **mon / tun follow the topic** (2026-10-10) [author, from economy test 01]: **mon** = the person being talked about, **tun** = another, non-topic one, loosely like Algonquian obviation (partly topic-driven, but also obligatory whenever a clause has two animate third persons, and purely grammatical for possessors; an analogy, not the source) *(corrected 2026-10-12 after the fact-check, C25: was "as Algonquian obviation does natively")*. Replaced a mechanical "most recent subject" rule [Claude, 2026-10-08] that made casual speakers keep score. The old rule rested on Hudson Kam & Newport (learners regularise *random* variation); topic-based reference isn't random.
- **bun** = the thing talked about; **fin** alone = "that one" (non-topic), **yin/fin** alone = an event or statement (discourse deixis is a basic use of demonstratives, Diessel 1999, 85 languages; common, not universal: some languages use a dedicated form) *(corrected 2026-10-12 after the fact-check, C26: was "in nearly every language")*.
- **kan / ta** inclusive/exclusive "we" [evidence]: among the most stable distinctions (Greenhill et al. 2017) and English's "we" is a real ambiguity. **son** logophor for reported speech. **nen** self.
- Add-back trigger for a mechanical rule: a blind test shows listeners losing track where one would have caught it.

### 4.5 Omission (language.md 4.5)
[author, yardstick layer 5] Anything context supplies may be left out, including verbs; fragments are grammatical. Natural parallels: argument drop (Mandarin, Japanese, Korean) and fragment answers (every language, English included). Those languages keep the verb in full clauses, so verb drop outside fragments goes beyond them. *(Corrected 2026-10-12 after the fact-check, C31: was "including verbs … (as in Mandarin, Japanese, Korean)".)* Economy test 01: the language was ~1.5× English's syllables; the four fixes (tan/ben alone, bu topic, wan/yi topic, omission) cut 12% with meaning 44/44 on retest.

### 4.6 Smaller rules worth knowing the reason for
- **Tense optional, aspect particles** (**ben li buntu**) [evidence]: creoles keep preverbal aspect particles; Bergen's native Esperanto children dropped compound tenses but didn't use aspect forms either, so only the tense half comes from them *(corrected 2026-10-12 after the fact-check, C17: was "native speakers and creoles keep preverbal aspect")*.
- **Three modalities kept apart** (ability **nebi**, obligation **kifa/wupi**, permission **efon**); epistemic "must/might" is confidence [evidence]: English *must/may* each mean two things.
- **Conditions use confidence** (**sin pe mu** = counterfactual) [Claude]: no new grammar words needed.
- **Clause attachment** (language.md 4.4) [blind test on contract language, `backtranslation-04-nesting`]: position decides attachment; commas mark pauses where a phrase could run on. No clause-closing word; add a two-syllable one if attachment fails in a test.
- **Comparison "more X from Y"** (**ke … don**) [Claude]: reuses existing words.
- **Time: anchor + offset, offset names its unit** [evidence]: relative-time metaphors ("move the meeting forward") are a documented ambiguity.

### 4.7 Politeness (`research/notes/politeness.md`)
## 7. Numbers, time, measurement

- **Base 10** [author, 2026-10-09]. History: base 6 was chosen first [Claude + evidence, `docs/number-base.md`, in git history]: a transmission test showed six one-syllable digits can be made far less confusable than ten (0.26% vs 2.3% wrong-digit rate for a 10-digit ID). The author ruled reality stays base 10; the 0–5 digits kept their forms. *Corrected 2026-10-12 after the review (`number-base.md`): those figures were for unconstrained ideal sets the language never used. The adopted 0–5 (**po fi sa ka da te**, chosen also to stay clear of grammar words) score 0.0185 per digit in the same unvalidated model, 8× the ideal ten-digit set the test rejected; today's 0–9 give 0.011 per digit, about 11% per 10-digit ID. Don't cite the 0.26% / 2.3% figures as findings.*
- **Digits 6–9 two syllables** (**ufo oma pepe tuli**): no free robust one-syllable forms (only **fe**, **nu**; closed syllables collided with **wi**). Built as content words pushed apart from each other and from number words.
- **No teens or irregular tens** (15 **yawu ma**, 50 **ma yawu**) [evidence]: English *fifteen/fifty* is a known confusion (documented for learners of English, not in aviation or phone research); regular numerals help children learn to count (Miller, Smith, Zhu & Zhang 1995) *(corrected 2026-10-12 after the fact-check, C45: was "a classic confusion")*.
- **Digit reading** for identifiers in groups of three [evidence: aviation reads digits one by one].
- **Calendar:** today's (7-day **nelon**, **dunan** month (the Moon has its own word from 2026-10-13), 24-hour clock read as digits); months and weekdays by number [author]. Points in time are labels (noun + **mena** + number; **pi** + number until 2026-10-12).
- **Senary** is a topic [author]: its words live in `data/senary.tsv` (described in the senary repo, `docs/apupiwe-vocabulary.md`), `data/units.tsv` and the senary rows of `numbers.tsv`; **senali** marks a senary number. Senary (its own repo, to be published) owns quantities; this repo owns all words, and senary uses them verbatim (r22–r23). Keep senary usable: it may be used for worldbuilding.

