- <span id="r-5-4-3"></span>A **pi** phrase after a derived word belongs to the last noun inside its base, or to the derived word itself if its base has no noun: **to meko pi wan** "my thing for keeping".
- <span id="r-5-4-4"></span>The base may be a verb phrase: **win isi don guyi** "one who goes from home", a traveller. It runs to the end of its phrase, like a relative clause, and a pause or **kiyu** separates it from a following verb or describing word: **to siten sokun** "a pan" (a thing for cooking flat), **to siten sokun kiyu tikun** "a hot pan"; without **kiyu**, **tikun** would join the phrase inside. It also ends before a name (**fo** …) or a gender word (**futa**, **lowu**), so **win wiwa fo mara** is "Teacher Mara" and **win wungu futa** "the leader, a woman".
- <span id="r-5-4-5"></span>A person something is done to is **ewo gi gun** + verb: **ewo gi gun wiwa** "a student".
- <span id="r-5-4-6"></span>A noun base is linked with **pi** or a verb: **sen pi natun** "an office", **win obu don yaye** "an alien".
- <span id="r-5-4-7"></span>For opposites, **su** + word means "not X" (**su upe** "not good"), and a separate opposite root names the other pole (**yomo** "bad").

## 6. Operators

### 6.1 Clause operators

<span id="r-6-1-1"></span>Clause operators start the clause (slot 5) and cover all of it.
- <span id="r-6-1-2"></span>Confidence is **pe** + a number of sixths, the chance in 6 that the statement is true: **pe mu** impossible, **pe ni** unlikely, **pe sa** doubtful, **pe de** even odds, **pe wo** likely, **pe ma** very likely, **pe ufo** certain; **pe ma mimi ma** 5.5 in 6; **pe kin** "maybe, possibly", with no particular chance in mind: **pe kin mon obu** "maybe she's coming". An unmarked statement is believed. An exact chance is a number out of a hundred: **pe de yawu pi loki mon obu** "30% that she's coming".
- <span id="r-6-1-3"></span>Source is optional: **wanme** seen, **inwun** told, **wemen** inferred.
- <span id="r-6-1-4"></span>"Might" and "must" in the sense of *probably* are a matter of confidence: "She must be home" is **pe ma mon gun no guyi**.

### 6.2 Phrase operators

<span id="r-6-2-1"></span>Phrase operators cover the word or phrase right after them, including a phrase that opens the sentence (**ga no loso, …** "only in the community, …"). Before the verb, they cover the predicate, which is the verb with everything after it in its clause. To negate a single phrase, put **su** before that phrase: **mon doku su no guyi** "she works, but not at home".
- <span id="r-6-2-2"></span>**su** not (**wan su yidu bun** "I don't/didn't take it"; **su su** emphatic). With **din** "none" it is still one negation, not two: **wan su ibe poka din** "I don't have any dogs";
- <span id="r-6-2-3"></span>**ga** only; **pon** also; **yon** even (**yon sin** "even if"); **min** focus, "this one, not another" (**min fo tomas ben yidu bun** "*Tomas* took it"; before the verb it insists: **wan min unfu** "I *did* pay");
- <span id="r-6-2-4"></span>to add a whole statement ("also, …"), start with **nun**; **pon** before the verb means the subject also does it;
- <span id="r-6-2-5"></span>**konsin** at least; **nedin** almost;
- <span id="r-6-2-6"></span>degree words **ke** more, **ten** very, **tason** rather (**ke tugu** "bigger"; **nasi ke uyo** "less money").

